//? Title: Chakravala: Exact Cyclic Steps for Pell's Equation
//? Subtitle: Minimum-norm multipliers, positive solutions, overflow-safe verification and explicit limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Pell's equation x²−Ny²=1 has integer solutions whose sizes vary dramatically
with N. The Chakravala method composes auxiliary triples a²−Nb²=k and divides
out |k|, choosing a congruent multiplier that minimises |m²−N|. We begin with
a two-step example, inspect the full path for N=13, retain the original famous
solutions and size plot, and measure actual cyclic updates. Canonical C++ now
records its actual choices, avoids oversized composition numerators through
quotient/remainder arithmetic, checks equations without overflowing squares,
and reports fixed-width or work-budget failures explicitly. Exact invariants,
minimality checks and finite-width limits are distinct parts of the analysis.
)

\toc

> N=7: two actual steps and a misleading nearest-root rule

Start at a=floor(√7)=2, b=1, k=−3. The first multiplier must satisfy
2+m=0 modulo 3. The positive candidates around √7 are 1 and 4.
Their squared errors are |1−7|=6 and |16−7|=9, so choose 1. Yet 4 is
closer to √7 in ordinary distance. Minimising |m²−N| is not equivalent
to minimising |m−√N|. This corrects the old report's multiplier description.

The first update gives (3,1,2); the next uses m=3 and gives (8,3,1).
Indeed, 8²−7·3²=64−63=1. The diagram and table come from actual
kernel records, without recomputing a separate explanatory path.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chakravala_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(7);
  if(trace.steps.size()!=2 || !dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(7,trace.solution)) return 1;
  std::print("| step | before (a,b,k) | multiplier | after (a,b,k) |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=4.5;node.height=2.3;node.font_size=20;
  diagram.node("initial",{0,0},std::format("a={}, b={}\nk={}",dm::algorithms::int128_text(trace.initial.a),dm::algorithms::int128_text(trace.initial.b),dm::algorithms::int128_text(trace.initial.k)),node);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& step=trace.steps[i];const auto& p=step.before;const auto& q=step.after;
    std::print("| {} | ({},{},{}) | {} | ({},{},{}) |\n",i+1,
        dm::algorithms::int128_text(p.a),dm::algorithms::int128_text(p.b),dm::algorithms::int128_text(p.k),step.choice.chosen,
        dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.a),dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.b),dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.k));
    if(i==1)node.fill={220,252,231};
    const auto id=std::format("state{}",i);
    diagram.node(id,{7.0*(i+1),0},std::format("a={}, b={}\nk={}",dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.a),dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.b),dm::algorithms::int128_text(q.k)),node)
        .edge(i?"state0":"initial",id,std::format("m={}",step.choice.chosen));
  }
  const auto& first=trace.steps[0].choice;
  diagram.text({7,2.4},"N=7: compose, divide by |k|, repeat until k=+1",20)
      .text({7,-2.4},std::format("first candidates {} and {}: squared errors {} and {}",first.below,first.above,
          dm::algorithms::uint128_text(first.below_error),dm::algorithms::uint128_text(first.above_error)),18)
      .save_svg("build/chakravala_method.svg");
}
```
