> Why the reduced composition is integral

Multiply a+b√N by m+√N. Its coordinates are am+Nb and a+bm, and
its norm is the product of the two norms. Therefore

$$
(am+Nb)^2-N(a+bm)^2=(a^2-Nb^2)(m^2-N)=k(m^2-N).
$$
\alttext(The norm of the composed pair equals the old norm k times m squared minus N.)

Let h=|k|. The maintained triple is primitive, so gcd(b,h)=1. Choosing
m=−a·b⁻¹ modulo h makes a+bm divisible by h. Also
b(am+Nb)=−a²+Nb²=−k modulo h, so the other numerator is divisible
by h. Squaring the congruence and cancelling b² proves h divides m²−N.
The reduced update is consequently integral:

$$
a'=(am+Nb)/|k|,\qquad b'=(a+bm)/|k|,\qquad k'=(m^2-N)/k.
$$
\alttext(The new coordinates are the composed numerators divided by the absolute old norm; the new signed norm divides m squared minus N by the signed old norm.)

Dividing the norm identity by k² proves a'²−Nb'²=k'. Moreover,
a·b'−b·a'=k/|k|=±1. Any common divisor of a' and b' would divide
one, so the new pair remains primitive and the next inverse exists.
This establishes exact arithmetic invariants. Halting and fundamental-solution
minimality require further theory; equation satisfaction alone proves neither.
The cited research treats the cyclic method's halting problem. Independent
continued-fraction checks below separately test minimality on a finite census.

Within one congruence class, positive m values increase in a fixed progression.
Below √N the objective N−m² decreases; above it, m²−N increases. Thus only
the two progression elements bracketing √N can minimise the objective.
If there is no positive lower element, take the upper one. Equal errors choose
the smaller positive multiplier. No floating approximation is needed.

> N=13: ties, negative norm one and the complete positive path

The native variant retains the old floor-root start (3,1,−4), continues until
k=+1, and uses no shortcuts at k=−1, ±2 or ±4. This differs from variants
starting at the nearest square or terminating at either sign of norm one.
The first candidates 1 and 5 both have squared error 12, so the tie rule
selects 1. At (18,5,−1), all positive multipliers are admissible: 4 has error
3, versus 3's error 4. The old special case incorrectly forced the floor root.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(13);
  std::print("| step | a | b | k | modulus | residue | lower m | upper m | selected m |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& s=trace.steps[i];const auto& c=s.choice;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i+1,
        dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.before.a),dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.before.b),dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.before.k),
        c.modulus,c.residue,c.below,c.above,c.chosen);
  }
  std::print("\nFinal: {}. Exact equation check: {}. Actual updates: {}.\n",trace.solution,
      dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(13,trace.solution),trace.steps.size());
  std::print("Nonfundamental (17,12) also solves N=2: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(2,{17,12,true}));
}
```
