Nonfundamental (17,12) also solves N=2: true.
// result_end

The negative-norm pair (18,5) could be squared directly:
(18+5√13)²=649+180√13, of norm +1. Historical shortcut rules exploit
such identities. This implementation instead performs three further recorded
cyclic updates, reaching the same pair. Neither |k| nor the coordinates shrink
at every step. For example, |k| rises from one back to three. Only the multiplier
objective is minimised locally; a and b can grow to enormous values.

The record (17,12) solves N=2 but is larger than (3,2). This executable
counterexample shows why a true equation check does not certify fundamental
minimality. Solver regression tests use a separate continued-fraction oracle.

> Famous solutions and the original size plot

Retain the original seven coefficients and add N=661. Its 38-digit x still
fits the signed 128-bit result type, but x² does not. Its largest unreduced
composition numerator is 246324873644488654125837607084057065735, which
also exceeds the signed limit even though the reduced result fits. The native checker avoids
forming x² or Ny²: it cancels common factors between (x−1)(x+1) and Ny·y.
Once both sides reduce completely to one, the original products were equal.
This also handles manually supplied signed endpoints without overflow.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chakravala.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| N | fundamental x | fundamental y | decimal digits of x | exact check |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto N:{2u,3u,5u,13u,29u,61u,109u,661u}) {
    const auto p=dm::algorithms::chakravala(N);const auto x=dm::algorithms::int128_text(p.x);
    const auto valid=dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(N,p);if(!valid)return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",N,x,dm::algorithms::int128_text(p.y),x.size(),valid);
  }
  std::vector<double> coefficients,log_x;
  for(std::uint64_t N=2;N<=100;++N) {
    const auto p=dm::algorithms::chakravala(N);if(!p.found)continue;
    coefficients.push_back(static_cast<double>(N));log_x.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(p.x)));
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(coefficients,log_x,"size",{37,99,235},1.3).points(coefficients,log_x,"size",{37,99,235},3)
      .legend({"log10(x)"},{{37,99,235}}).title("Pell solutions: irregular size for N=2..100")
      .x_label("nonsquare N").y_label("log10(fundamental x)").save_svg("build/chakravala.svg");
  std::print("\nOriginal range: {} nonsquare coefficients. The plotted log10 values are approximate, not integer digit counts.\n",coefficients.size());
}
```
