\image(figures/chakravala_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual update counts against exact answer digits; this measures the implemented floor-root, no-shortcut variant.)
\alttext(A scatter plot compares exact decimal answer length with executed cyclic updates for each nonsquare coefficient through one thousand.)

The original claim that all intermediate coordinates stay near √N is false:
the final coordinates themselves can be much larger. Small auxiliary norms
and locally selected multipliers do not imply small answers. Nor does a finite
count plot establish a bound proportional to continued-fraction period length.
The implementation stores signed 128-bit coordinates and fixed-width modular
values; it is not an arbitrary-precision Pell solver.

For the composition (a·m+N·b)/h, write each coordinate as q·h+r.
A product divided by h becomes q·m+floor(r·m/h), with a separate remainder.
Combining the two remainders supplies the final carry. Since all terms are
nonnegative, any checked quotient overflow implies that the reduced coordinate
cannot fit. This avoids rejecting a representable result merely because an
unreduced numerator is too large. Range checks happen before dangerous products
or sums; no wrapped pair is returned as a purported solution.

> Explicit failures, endpoint coefficients and reproducibility

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  bool overflow=false,budget=false,zero_budget=false;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chakravala(1021);}catch(const std::overflow_error&){overflow=true;}
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(61,13);}catch(const std::length_error&){budget=true;}
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(2,0);}catch(const std::length_error&){zero_budget=true;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(61,14);
  const auto square=dm::algorithms::chakravala_trace(49,0);
  const auto endpoint=dm::algorithms::chakravala(18446744073709551615ULL);
  if(!overflow || !budget || !zero_budget || !dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(18446744073709551615ULL,endpoint))return 1;
  std::print("N=1021 rejects signed-128 overflow: {}. N=61 rejects a 13-update cap: {}.\n",overflow,budget);
  std::print("N=61 succeeds with {} updates, x={}.\n",exact.steps.size(),dm::algorithms::int128_text(exact.solution.x));
  std::print("Zero budget rejects N=2: {}; square N=49 returns found={} with {} updates.\n",zero_budget,square.solution.found,square.steps.size());
  std::print("UINT64_MAX coefficient: x={}, y={}, exact check={}.\n",dm::algorithms::int128_text(endpoint.x),dm::algorithms::int128_text(endpoint.y),
      dm::algorithms::pell_is_solution(18446744073709551615ULL,endpoint));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
N=1021 rejects signed-128 overflow: true. N=61 rejects a 13-update cap: true.
N=61 succeeds with 14 updates, x=1766319049.
Zero budget rejects N=2: true; square N=49 returns found=false with 0 updates.
UINT64_MAX coefficient: x=4294967296, y=1, exact check=true.
// result_end

A step budget limits completed cyclic updates, including zero; a failure returns
no partial trace. The ordinary legacy-signature solver permits 100,000 updates
and throws if that limit is reached. Squares, including zero and one, retain
the {0,0,false} sentinel. Other range failures throw instead of overloading
found=false with a partial or numerically corrupted result. All computation is
deterministic integer arithmetic, with separate owned traces and no shared state.

Native tests compare every coefficient through 10,000 and 1,000 further seeded
coefficients through one million against independently generated continued-
fraction convergents. A separate Python big-integer oracle checks the exported
actual triples, divisibility, minimum-error choices and complete answers. These
are development checks, not a Python dependency of the report. Boundary squares
and neighbours cover the full uint64 input range; exact budgets, 128-bit text
endpoints, copied records, malformed pairs, the nonfundamental solution example
and the original number-theory/formatting regressions are checked separately.

> References

- A. Bauval (2014). [An elementary proof of the halting property for Chakravala algorithm](https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.6809). Minimum-norm congruence choices and the distinction between a local norm identity and a global halting argument. The native variant retains its floor-root start and continues to positive norm one.
- Sathaye (2018). [Note on Chakravala Method of Bhaskara II](https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/ma330/files/chakravala.pdf). Composition and reduced integral triples; the note also discusses shortcut rules that this kernel does not use.
